  "evidence": "1) Quote-vs-claim gap: the source quote is a parenthetical descriptive aside — 'Inter, Roboto, or Arial (the \"safe\" system fonts that appear in thousands of examples)' — not a stated causal mechanism. The claim under review upgrades this into a firm causal assertion ('...because these are the safe fonts that appear thousands of times in training examples'), adding the word 'training' that isn't in the quoted text itself; the causal framing is the article author's own unsupported inference elsewhere in the piece ('median of every Tailwind CSS tutorial scraped from GitHub 2019-2024'), not a cited fact. 2) Source quality is insufficient for a claim of this strength: prg.sh explicitly self-describes as a 'digital garden of ramblings, notes, and half-built experiments' / 'long-form thinking, evolving views, and occasional rants' — i.e., an admittedly informal, unsourced personal blog, not a named design critic, practitioner with credentials, or anyone with actual visibility into LLM training-data composition. No training-corpus analysis, no statement from an AI lab, no citation is given for the 'appears thousands of times' frequency claim — it's speculation presented as mechanism. A claim purporting to explain WHY a model behaves a certain way (training-data statistics) is a technical/extraordinary claim that needs primary sourcing (e.g., disclosed training data stats or an ML researcher's statement), which this source doesn't provide. 3) A more mundane, independently verifiable competing explanation exists and is not addressed by the source: Inter is the literal hardcoded default font in shadcn/ui (confirmed via GitHub issues/docs — shadcn/ui uses next/font/google to inject Inter by default), and shadcn/ui is one of the most common component libraries AI coding tools (v0, Lovable, Claude/Cursor scaffolds) scaffold against. This means Inter's ubiquity in AI output could just as plausibly stem from literal tool/template defaults rather than 'appearing thousands of times in training examples' — the source's causal story is unproven folklore, not an established fact.",
